{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And He said, 'For I will be with you, and this is the sign for you'</b> The interpretations of this verse are numerous. The correct one according to the plain sense is that HaKadosh Baruch Hu said two things to Moses: that he would go down to save them from the hand of Egypt, and it would have been possible to save them from their hand in the land of Goshen itself or nearby from there, but He further promised to bring them up from that entire land to the place of the Canaanite (Exodus 3:8).<br>And Moses was afraid of both of these, and he said, 'Who am I that I should go to Pharaoh' — I am a lowly man, a shepherd of sheep, and he is a great king, and if I say to him to let all the people go he will kill me, as Samuel said, 'And Saul will hear and kill me' (I Samuel 16:2). And he further said, 'Who am I that I should bring the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt,' as You said to me to bring them up to the land of Canaan — for this is a wise and understanding nation, this great people, and they will not consider following me to a land of peoples greater and mightier than they, as You said to bring them up to a good and spacious land, to the place of the Canaanite.<br>For deliverance from Pharaoh's hand does not depend on them: if Pharaoh hears, he will lighten their yoke from upon them and save them, or he will drive them from his land against their will. And furthermore, they themselves will listen to any person in this matter, for what man would not wish to escape from harsh servitude like which there is none — but the ascent to the land of the Canaanite, they will not obey. And so indeed it was, that the war against those peoples was hard for them from beginning to end, and their dread of it persisted in Egypt and in the wilderness.<br>This is the fear of Moses our master concerning Pharaoh, and his dread concerning them, and to both of them HaShem answered him. He said to him: Do not fear Pharaoh, <b>for</b> I <b>will be with you</b> to save you; <b>and this is the sign for you</b> to the people, <b>that I have sent you</b> to them — <b>for when you bring the people out of Egypt, you shall serve Elohim on this mountain</b>. And from then on they will accept the service of HaShem to follow His commandments, 'and also in you they will believe forever' (Exodus 19:9), and after you they will run to every place that You command them. And behold, I revealed Myself to you on this mountain in a flame of fire, for so it will be in the eyes of all the people when they serve Me on this mountain.<br>And behold, for Moses there is a sign in that he need not fear Pharaoh, since He promised to save him; and for Israel there will be a sign for them that they need not fear the peoples when they come to Mount Sinai — for to leave Egypt with Pharaoh's consent to a nearby place, a three days' journey, to sacrifice to Elohim, they will surely obey and do so, whether willingly or unwillingly. And by way of truth, <b>'and this is the sign for you'</b> is like 'this has been to me twenty years' (Genesis 31:41). And He said: <b>for I will be with you</b>, and behold, this is the sign for you — the sign of the covenant that I will be with you always, in the manner of 'Behold, My covenant is with you' (Genesis 17:4), for I am the One sending you that they shall serve Elohim on this mountain, and then I Myself will also go up in the midst of this people to the place which I have prepared (Exodus 23:20)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The closing kabbalistic passage ('and by way of truth') is rendered literally and left cryptic per translational policy."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:02:01.112872772Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}